            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Spiritual rest in Christ, salvation from works-righteousness, and the ultimate eschatological rest for the people of God in the new creation.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Hebrews 4:1-11\",\"Colossians 2:16-17\",\"Genesis 2:2-3\",\"Matthew 11:28-30\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A perpetual sign of covenant loyalty, holiness, and moral obedience, often observed practically on Sunday (the Lord's Day) or Saturday.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 20:8-11\",\"Exodus 31:13-17\",\"Isaiah 58:13-14\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A typological symbol of the millennial kingdom, representing a future one-thousand-year era of peace corresponding to the seventh 'day' of human history.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Peter 3:8\",\"Revelation 20:1-6\",\"Psalm 90:4\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A paradigm and symbol of social justice, liberation from oppression, and economic equity, connected to the Jubilee.\",\"basis\":[\"Deuteronomy 5:12-15\",\"Leviticus 25:1-55\",\"Luke 4:16-19\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the literal practice and ongoing moral obligation of the Sabbath is debated among traditions, its typological and symbolic meaning as pointing to spiritual and eschatological rest in Christ enjoys a strong theological consensus.\"}",
